  "grammar_points": [
    {
      "name": "〜にも...ない (not even / no one can)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N3",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Combine a question word (誰/dare = who, 何/nani = what, どこ/doko = where) with にも (ni mo) and a negative verb. This creates absolute negation — 'no one', 'nothing', 'nowhere'.\n\nExample from this song: 誰にも見れない (dare ni mo mirenai) = 'no one can see' — from 誰 (dare, 'who') + にも (ni mo, 'even to') + 見れない (mirenai, 'cannot see')\n\nMore examples:\n• 何も食べない (nani mo tabenai, 'eat nothing / don't eat anything')\n• どこにも行かない (doko ni mo ikanai, 'don't go anywhere')\n• 誰にも言わない (dare ni mo iwanai, 'won't tell anyone')\n\nNote: The particle も (mo) combined with a negative verb creates total negation. Without the negative, も means 'also/even'. 誰にも (dare ni mo) + positive = 'to everyone'; + negative = 'to no one'."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "誰 (dare, 'who') + にも (ni mo, 'even to') + 見れない (mirenai, 'cannot see') = 誰にも見れない (dare ni mo mirenai, 'no one can see')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜ても concessive (even if)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N4",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Take the て-form (te-form) of a verb and add も (mo). This creates 'even if' — the outcome remains the same regardless of the condition.\n\nExample from this song: とられても (toraretemo) = 'even if taken away' — from とられる (torareru, 'to be taken', passive) → とられて (torarete, te-form) + も (mo)\n\nMore examples:\n• 失敗する (shippai suru, 'to fail') → 失敗しても (shippai shitemo, 'even if I fail')\n• 雨が降る (ame ga furu, 'it rains') → 雨が降っても (ame ga futtemo, 'even if it rains')\n• 難しい (muzukashii, 'difficult') → 難しくても (muzukashikutemo, 'even if it's difficult')\n\nNote: This is one of the most common concessive patterns in Japanese. For い-adjectives, change い to くても. For な-adjectives/nouns, use でも (demo)."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "とられる (torareru, 'to be taken') → とられて (torarete, te-form) → とられても (toraretemo, 'even if taken away')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜きれない (can't completely)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N3",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Take the verb masu-stem and add きれない (kirenai). This means 'cannot completely do' or 'unable to do all of'. きる (kiru) as an auxiliary means 'to do completely'.\n\nExample from this song: 押さえきれない (osae kirenai) = 'can't hold back completely' — from 押さえる (osaeru, 'to hold back') → 押さえ (osae, masu-stem) + きれない (kirenai, 'can't completely')\n\nMore examples:\n• 食べる (taberu, 'to eat') → 食べきれない (tabe kirenai, 'can't eat it all')\n• 数える (kazoeru, 'to count') → 数えきれない (kazoe kirenai, 'too many to count')\n• 言う (iu, 'to say') → 言いきれない (ii kirenai, 'can't say everything')\n\nNote: The positive form きる (kiru) means 'to do completely': 食べきる (tabe kiru, 'eat it all'). The negative きれない expresses the impossibility of completing the action."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "押さえる (osaeru, 'to hold back') → 押さえ (osae, masu-stem) + きれない (kirenai, 'can't completely') → 押さえきれない (osae kirenai, 'can't completely hold back')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜から (because)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N5",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Place から (kara) at the end of a clause to express a reason — 'because'. It follows verbs, adjectives, or nouns (with だ/da).\n\nExample from this song: The lyrics use から to explain motivations and reasons for the speaker's feelings and actions.\n\nMore examples:\n• 暑い (atsui, 'hot') → 暑いから (atsui kara, 'because it's hot')\n• 好きだ (suki da, 'like') → 好きだから (suki da kara, 'because I like it')\n• 行く (iku, 'to go') → 行くから (iku kara, 'because I'm going')\n\nNote: から is more subjective and personal compared to ので (node), which sounds more objective and polite. In casual speech, から is much more common. The reason comes before から, and the result follows."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "[reason clause] + から (kara, 'because') → [result clause]"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜ほど...ない (not to the extent)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N3",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Use ほど (hodo, 'to the extent') with a negative verb to say 'not as much as' or 'not to the point of'. It sets an upper limit that isn't reached.\n\nExample from this song: The lyrics use this pattern to express that something doesn't reach a certain degree or intensity.\n\nMore examples:\n• 死ぬ (shinu, 'to die') → 死ぬほどではない (shinu hodo dewa nai, 'not to the point of dying')\n• 彼 (kare, 'he') → 彼ほど強くない (kare hodo tsuyokunai, 'not as strong as him')\n• 昨日 (kinou, 'yesterday') → 昨日ほど寒くない (kinou hodo samukunai, 'not as cold as yesterday')\n\nNote: ほど alone means 'to the extent that': 泣くほど嬉しい (naku hodo ureshii, 'so happy I could cry'). Adding ない makes it 'not to that extent'. This is useful for softened comparisons."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "[noun/verb] + ほど (hodo, 'to the extent') + [negative] = 'not as much as...'"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜ぬ archaic negative",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N2",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Replace the modern negative ending ない (nai) with ぬ (nu). This is the classical Japanese negative, used in literary contexts, poetry, and song lyrics for a dramatic or elevated tone.\n\nExample from this song: 知らぬ (shiranu) = 'not knowing' — from 知る (shiru, 'to know') → 知らない (shiranai, modern negative) → 知らぬ (shiranu, archaic negative)\n\nMore examples:\n• 見る (miru, 'to see') → 見えぬ (mienu, 'cannot see')\n• 言う (iu, 'to say') → 言わぬ (iwanu, 'do not say')\n• 消える (kieru, 'to disappear') → 消えぬ (kienu, 'does not disappear')\n\nNote: ぬ is the attributive form (can modify nouns directly). The base form is ず (zu): 知らず (shirazu, 'without knowing'). Both appear in anime songs for poetic gravity."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "知る (shiru, 'to know') → 知らない (shiranai, modern negative) → 知らぬ (shiranu, archaic negative, 'not knowing')"
    }
  ]
